// SQL Notes - deep rewrite for all SQL topics (detailed multi-paragraph notes)
module.exports = [
  { topic: 'SQL Introduction', title: 'SQL Introduction', content: 'SQL (Structured Query Language) is the standard language for relational databases: you declare WHAT data you want, not HOW to traverse it - the query optimiser decides the execution plan, which is the fundamental contrast with imperative programming. SQL was standardised by ANSI/ISO (SQL-86 through SQL:2023) and every mainstream database - MySQL, PostgreSQL, SQL Server, Oracle - implements it with dialect quirks in functions and types. Data lives in tables of rows (records) and columns (attributes), where every row is uniquely identifiable by a key, and tables relate through shared values.\n\nSQL divides into sublanguages, and interviewers love asking you to classify statements: DDL (CREATE, ALTER, DROP, TRUNCATE - define schema objects), DML (SELECT... well, SELECT is DQL; INSERT, UPDATE, DELETE - manipulate rows), DQL (SELECT - query), DCL (GRANT, REVOKE - permissions), and TCL (COMMIT, ROLLBACK, SAVEPOINT - transactions). Two more distinctions worth naming: SQL is case-insensitive about keywords (SELECT = select) but identifiers and string data are case-sensitive depending on collation, and statements end with semicolons.\n\nThe relational model underneath (Codd, 1970) explains why SQL works: tables are sets, so results have no guaranteed order without ORDER BY, duplicate rows can appear unless you eliminate them with DISTINCT, and NULL means unknown, not zero or empty string - three facts that produce most interview edge cases. Interview framing: define SQL, name the five sublanguages with one statement each, then state the set semantics of relational algebra.', keyPoints: ['Declarative: say what, the optimiser plans how', 'Sublanguages: DDL, DML, DQL, DCL, TCL', 'Tables are SETS: no order without ORDER BY, duplicates unless DISTINCT', 'NULL = unknown; it is not 0 or empty string', 'Standardised SQL with vendor dialects (MySQL, PostgreSQL, Oracle)'], commonMistakes: ['Classifying SELECT as DML in an interview - call it DQL and mention the debate', 'Assuming result order is stable without ORDER BY'], interviewTips: ['Recite all five sublanguages with an example statement each', 'Explain set semantics and the NULL-is-unknown point early'] },
  { topic: 'DDL', title: 'DDL - Data Definition Language', content: 'DDL defines and changes schema objects - tables, views, indexes, constraints. CREATE TABLE names columns with types and constraints (CREATE TABLE emp (id INT PRIMARY KEY, name VARCHAR(100) NOT NULL, dept_id INT REFERENCES dept(id))); ALTER TABLE adds or drops columns and constraints later; DROP TABLE removes the table AND its data and structure entirely; TRUNCATE TABLE removes ALL rows quickly but keeps the structure. Knowing exactly how the four differ on speed, rollback and triggers is the classic interview grid.\n\nThe TRUNCATE vs DELETE vs DROP comparison to memorise: DELETE is DML, row-by-row, logged, CAN be rolled back in a transaction, fires triggers, can take a WHERE clause; TRUNCATE is DDL, deallocates data pages so it is far faster, usually cannot be rolled back in MySQL (it can in PostgreSQL/SQL Server inside a transaction), fires no row-level triggers, resets identity counters, and has no WHERE; DROP removes the table definition itself - data, structure, indexes, constraints, permissions. Sequencing matters: truncate or delete the rows first, then drop if the table must disappear.\n\nOther DDL facts that come up: most databases auto-commit DDL (a PostgreSQL ALTER wraps in its own transaction; MySQL commits implicitly before and after), so mixing DDL into long transactions is risky; and ALTER on huge tables can lock them - online schema change tools exist for that. Interview framing: name the commands, draw the DELETE/TRUNCATE/DROP grid, then the auto-commit caveat.', keyPoints: ['CREATE/ALTER/DROP define schema objects', 'DELETE: DML, logged, rollback-able, fires triggers, WHERE allowed', 'TRUNCATE: DDL, fast page deallocation, resets identity, no WHERE', 'DROP: removes structure, data, indexes, everything', 'DDL often auto-commits - avoid mixing with long transactions'], commonMistakes: ['Saying TRUNCATE can always be rolled back - engine-dependent', 'Claiming TRUNCATE fires row-level triggers - it does not'], interviewTips: ['Present DELETE vs TRUNCATE vs DROP as a comparison grid', 'Mention the huge-table ALTER locking caveat for seniority points'] },
  { topic: 'DML', title: 'DML - Data Manipulation Language', content: 'DML changes the DATA inside tables: INSERT adds rows, UPDATE modifies them, DELETE removes them. INSERT comes in two shapes: single row (INSERT INTO emp (id, name) VALUES (1, A)) and multi-row (VALUES (...), (...), (...)), which is far faster than looping single inserts because it makes one round trip and one transaction; INSERT ... SELECT copies from another table, and many dialects add INSERT ... ON CONFLICT/ON DUPLICATE KEY for upserts. UPDATE takes SET and usually WHERE: UPDATE emp SET salary = salary * 1.1 WHERE dept_id = 5 - omitting WHERE changes every row, the mistake everyone makes once. DELETE mirrors it: DELETE FROM emp WHERE id = 7.\n\nSemantics interviewers probe: DML statements are transactional - wrap multi-statement changes in BEGIN/COMMIT with ROLLBACK on error, and verify with SELECT before committing. UPDATE and DELETE that violate constraints fail (a referenced child row blocks DELETE unless ON DELETE CASCADE); NOT NULL and CHECK constraints reject bad writes. Row counts are returned (affected rows), which application code should check. Bulk operations have performance profiles: millions of row-by-row DELETEs are slow and log-heavy - batch them, or move to TRUNCATE/CREATE-SELECT when semantics allow.\n\nAlso know the modern complements: MERGE (SQL Server) / upsert syntax for insert-or-update in one statement, and RETURNING (PostgreSQL) to get generated ids back without a second query. Interview framing: the three verbs with syntax, transactionality, constraint interactions, then bulk-operation performance.', keyPoints: ['INSERT, UPDATE, DELETE manipulate row data', 'Multi-row INSERT beats row-by-row loops', 'UPDATE/DELETE without WHERE hit every row', 'DML is transactional: BEGIN, verify, COMMIT/ROLLBACK', 'Constraints reject violating writes; FKs can cascade'], commonMistakes: ['Running UPDATE without WHERE and without a transaction', 'Expecting DELETE to succeed on parent rows with existing children'], interviewTips: ['Show multi-row INSERT and upsert syntax from memory', 'Describe your safe bulk-update workflow (tx, check, commit)'] },
  { topic: 'DQL', title: 'DQL - Data Query Language (SELECT)', content: 'SELECT is the only DQL statement and the one you will be judged on. Logical evaluation order is the foundation: FROM and JOINs first (build the row set), WHERE filters rows, GROUP BY forms groups, HAVING filters groups, SELECT projects expressions, DISTINCT removes duplicates, ORDER BY sorts, LIMIT/OFFSET pages - memorising this order explains most syntax errors, like why you cannot use a SELECT alias inside WHERE (it has not run yet).\n\nCore mechanics: SELECT cols or * (avoid * in production - breaks contracts and wastes I/O), arithmetic and string expressions, column aliases with AS, WHERE with comparison and logical operators, IN, BETWEEN, LIKE with % and _ wildcards, IS NULL (never = NULL, because NULL comparisons yield UNKNOWN - a three-valued logic where TRUE, FALSE, UNKNOWN exist and WHERE only passes TRUE), and ORDER BY multiple keys with ASC/DESC. LIMIT with OFFSET paginates; keyset pagination (WHERE id > last ORDER BY id LIMIT n) is the scalable answer interviewers like.\n\nNULL traps deserve their own sentence set: NULL = NULL is UNKNOWN, COUNT(col) skips NULLs while COUNT(*) counts rows, and NOT IN with a NULL in the list returns nothing - these three edge cases are asked constantly. Interview framing: the evaluation order, then filtering basics, then the NULL traps as live demonstrations.', keyPoints: ['Evaluation: FROM -> WHERE -> GROUP BY -> HAVING -> SELECT -> ORDER BY', 'SELECT aliases are invisible to WHERE but visible to ORDER BY', 'Three-valued logic: NULL comparisons give UNKNOWN', 'IS NULL for testing; = NULL never matches', 'COUNT(col) skips NULLs; NOT IN with NULL returns empty'], commonMistakes: ['Using = NULL instead of IS NULL', 'Using a SELECT alias inside WHERE'], interviewTips: ['Recite the evaluation order, then prove it with an alias example', 'Demonstrate all three NULL traps in one query each'] },
  { topic: 'WHERE', title: 'WHERE Clause', content: 'WHERE filters individual rows before grouping. Its operators: comparisons (=, <>, >, <, >=, <=), logical AND/OR/NOT with parentheses for clarity, IN for membership lists, BETWEEN (inclusive on both ends), LIKE for pattern matching with % (any string) and _ (one character), and IS NULL / IS NOT NULL for the unknown case. Precedence traps: AND binds tighter than OR, so WHERE a = 1 OR a = 2 AND b = 3 means a = 1 OR (a = 2 AND b = 3) - always parenthesise mixed conditions.\n\nSargability is the performance angle interviewers want: a predicate is sargable (search-ARGument-able) when the optimiser can use an index on the column. Wrapping the column in a function kills it - WHERE YEAR(order_date) = 2024 scans everything, while WHERE order_date >= 2024-01-01 AND order_date < 2025-01-01 uses the index; leading-wildcard LIKE %term never uses a B-tree index, while term% does; implicit type conversion (comparing a varchar column to a number) can silently cast the column and drop the index too.\n\nNULL semantics produce the classic bugs: WHERE col != 5 silently drops NULL rows because NULL != 5 is UNKNOWN, not TRUE; NOT IN (subquery) with any NULL in the subquery result returns zero rows - use NOT EXISTS instead. Interview framing: operators and precedence, then sargable patterns with a rewrite example, then the NULL edge cases.', keyPoints: ['Filters rows before GROUP BY', 'AND binds tighter than OR - parenthesise', 'BETWEEN is inclusive; IN for lists; LIKE with % and _', 'Sargable: no functions on the indexed column, no leading %', 'col != 5 drops NULL rows; NOT IN + NULL = empty result'], commonMistakes: ['Writing functions around indexed columns and wondering why it is slow', 'Forgetting inclusive BETWEEN endpoints'], interviewTips: ['Rewrite YEAR(col) = 2024 as a range predicate live', 'Explain three-valued logic with the != example'] },
  { topic: 'SELECT', title: 'SELECT Deep Dive', content: 'SELECT projects the result: chosen columns, expressions (salary * 12 AS annual), literals, and computed CASE expressions. Aliases with AS make outputs readable - and remember visibility: a SELECT alias can be used in ORDER BY but not in WHERE or GROUP BY (they evaluate earlier; GROUP BY in PostgreSQL also cannot see it, MySQL tolerates it). SELECT * is for ad-hoc exploration; production queries name columns explicitly so schema changes do not break consumers and unused column reads do not waste bandwidth.\n\nCombining results: a scalar subquery can appear in the SELECT list when it returns one value per row; correlated expressions can compute per-row values. TOP n / LIMIT n / FETCH FIRST n ROWS differ per dialect - knowing the portability story is a plus. DISTINCT applies AFTER projection, so SELECT DISTINCT dept, city deduplicates the PAIR, not each column - a common misunderstanding.\n\nThe structured way to answer any SELECT question: state which tables feed it (FROM/JOIN), which rows survive (WHERE), how they aggregate (GROUP BY/HAVING), what appears (SELECT), in what order (ORDER BY), and how many (LIMIT). Interviewers often hand you a schema and ask for a query; thinking aloud in that pipeline both gets the answer and demonstrates method. Interview framing: projection and expressions, alias visibility rules, DISTINCT-after-projection, then the evaluation pipeline as your method.', keyPoints: ['SELECT projects columns and expressions; alias with AS', 'Alias visible to ORDER BY, invisible to WHERE/GROUP BY', 'Avoid SELECT * in production code', 'DISTINCT deduplicates the projected row, not columns', 'Dialect LIMIT: LIMIT/OFFSET, TOP, FETCH FIRST'], commonMistakes: ['Expecting DISTINCT to deduplicate one column independently', 'Using SELECT aliases inside WHERE'], interviewTips: ['Answer query questions by narrating the evaluation pipeline', 'Show a CASE expression inside SELECT for conditional columns'] },
  { topic: 'DISTINCT', title: 'DISTINCT', content: 'DISTINCT removes duplicate rows from the result - and the row means the ENTIRE projected row. SELECT DISTINCT city FROM emp returns each city once; SELECT DISTINCT dept, city returns each unique COMBINATION, which surprises people expecting per-column behaviour. Duplicates arise naturally from joins that multiply matches and from projections that drop the distinguishing columns, so DISTINCT is often a symptom: if you need DISTINCT to make a join sane, check whether a join condition is missing or too loose first.\n\nImplementation cost is the interview angle: deduplication requires either sorting the result or hashing it, both memory and CPU heavy on large sets, and both block streaming output - the engine must see all rows before emitting the first. Alternatives that scale better: GROUP BY when you are aggregating anyway, EXISTS instead of a join-plus-DISTINCT when you only need to know a match exists, or DISTINCT ON (PostgreSQL) for first-row-per-group semantics.\n\nRelated clauses worth one line each: ALL is the default (SELECT ALL keeps duplicates), COUNT(DISTINCT col) counts unique values inside an aggregate, and UNION (vs UNION ALL) implicitly applies DISTINCT across two result sets. Interview framing: whole-row semantics with a two-column example, the cost model (sort or hash, no streaming), then when DISTINCT is a design smell versus legitimate.', keyPoints: ['Removes duplicate whole rows after projection', 'Multi-column DISTINCT deduplicates combinations', 'Cost: sort or hash - output cannot stream', 'Often signals a missing/loose join condition', 'COUNT(DISTINCT col) and UNION also deduplicate'], commonMistakes: ['Sprinkling DISTINCT to hide join explosions instead of fixing them', 'Believing DISTINCT on one column of many works per-column'], interviewTips: ['Show the (dept, city) combination example', 'Explain why DISTINCT prevents streaming and what to use instead'] },
  { topic: 'Aggregate Functions', title: 'Aggregate Functions (COUNT, SUM, AVG, MIN, MAX)', content: 'Aggregates collapse many rows into one value per group: COUNT, SUM, AVG, MIN, MAX. The NULL rules are the interview core: all aggregates except COUNT(*) IGNORE NULLs - AVG(col) divides by the count of non-NULL values, not by row count; COUNT(*) counts rows, COUNT(col) counts non-NULL values, COUNT(DISTINCT col) counts distinct non-NULL values. SUM/AVG of an all-NULL group return NULL, not 0 - wrap with COALESCE when zero is wanted.\n\nAggregates combine with GROUP BY (groups) and HAVING (filters groups - WHERE cannot see aggregate results because WHERE runs before grouping): SELECT dept, AVG(salary) FROM emp GROUP BY dept HAVING AVG(salary) > 50000. The rule that follows: every SELECT column must be either in GROUP BY or inside an aggregate - the notorious ONLY_FULL_GROUP_BY error in MySQL enforces it, and functional dependency exceptions exist in newer standards. Without GROUP BY, mixing an aggregate with bare columns is invalid in strict modes.\n\nPerformance and nuance: MIN/MAX are O(1) on a B-tree index on that column (first/last entry), COUNT(*) on large tables is a full scan or index scan unless a filtered index helps, and AVG requires reading all values. Window functions are the modern alternative when you need aggregates WITHOUT collapsing rows (AVG OVER (PARTITION BY dept)) - knowing when to reach for either is a strong signal. Interview framing: the five functions, the NULL rules precisely, GROUP BY/HAVING legality, then index-aware MIN/MAX.', keyPoints: ['COUNT(*) counts rows; COUNT(col) skips NULLs', 'AVG divides by non-NULL count; SUM of all-NULL is NULL', 'Every selected column: grouped or aggregated', 'HAVING filters groups; WHERE runs before grouping', 'MIN/MAX hit index endpoints; COUNT(*) scans'], commonMistakes: ['Expecting AVG to count NULL rows in the denominator', 'Selecting a bare column with aggregates in strict GROUP BY modes'], interviewTips: ['State the NULL rules as a numbered list from memory', 'Contrast GROUP BY collapse vs OVER window aggregation'] },
  { topic: 'GROUP BY', title: 'GROUP BY', content: 'GROUP BY partitions rows into groups by equal column values, so aggregates compute per group: SELECT dept_id, COUNT(*), AVG(salary) FROM emp GROUP BY dept_id returns one row per department. Multiple columns group by the COMBINATION (GROUP BY dept_id, city makes a group per dept-city pair), and you can group by expressions (GROUP BY DATE(created_at) for per-day totals). The legality rule: after GROUP BY, every selected column must be either listed in GROUP BY or wrapped in an aggregate - strict engines enforce this, MySQL 5.7+ defaults ONLY_FULL_GROUP_BY on.\n\nThe subtlety interviewers probe: WHERE vs GROUP BY vs HAVING timing. WHERE filters rows BEFORE grouping (cannot use aggregates), HAVING filters groups AFTER (can). Needing HAVING COUNT(*) > 1 to find duplicates (SELECT email FROM users GROUP BY email HAVING COUNT(*) > 1) is the canonical duplicate-finder query - know it cold. Another one: grouping by the primary key makes every other column functionally dependent, so SELECT * with GROUP BY id is legal in engines that recognise functional dependencies.\n\nNULL grouping surprises: GROUP BY treats all NULLs as ONE group (unlike = comparisons), which is usually desired. Performance: grouping typically sorts or hashes the input; an index on the grouping columns can satisfy the order directly (index-order grouping), and filtering with WHERE first reduces the group input dramatically - the practical tip is filter early, group late. Interview framing: definition and multi-column grouping, the legality rule, WHERE/HAVING timing with the duplicate query, then NULL and performance notes.', keyPoints: ['Partitions rows; one output row per group', 'Multi-column GROUP BY groups combinations', 'Selected columns must be grouped or aggregated', 'WHERE pre-filters rows; HAVING post-filters groups', 'All NULLs form one group; filter early for speed'], commonMistakes: ['Using aggregate functions inside WHERE', 'Expecting NULLs to be excluded from grouping - they form their own group'], interviewTips: ['Write the duplicate-email query without hesitation', 'Explain WHERE vs HAVING with evaluation order'] },
  { topic: 'HAVING', title: 'HAVING', content: 'HAVING filters GROUPS after aggregation - it exists because WHERE runs before grouping and cannot see aggregate results. The pattern: SELECT dept_id, AVG(salary) FROM emp GROUP BY dept_id HAVING AVG(salary) > 50000. You can also use it without GROUP BY when the whole table is one group (SELECT COUNT(*) FROM emp HAVING COUNT(*) > 0), though that is rare.\n\nThe decisions interviewers test: first, can this predicate live in WHERE instead? Filtering AVG(salary) > 50000 must be HAVING; filtering dept_id = 5 should be WHERE - pushing conditions into WHERE shrinks the input to the sort/hash that grouping needs, so correct placement is a performance decision, not just syntax. Second, alias visibility: HAVING cannot reference SELECT aliases in most engines (evaluated before SELECT) - repeat the expression or use a subquery. Third, HAVING with conditions on multiple aggregates (HAVING COUNT(*) > 10 AND AVG(salary) < 90000) is legal and common.\n\nThe trap questions: HAVING COUNT(*) > 1 finds duplicates; HAVING over a NULL-heavy column behaves per aggregate NULL rules (COUNT(col) skips NULLs, so a group with only NULLs gives 0). And the conceptual one-liner: WHERE filters WHAT you group; HAVING filters WHAT you keep after aggregating. Interview framing: why HAVING exists (evaluation order), placement for performance, alias rules, then the duplicate-finder as your demo.', keyPoints: ['Filters groups after GROUP BY - aggregates allowed', 'WHERE runs earlier: put row filters there, group filters here', 'WHERE = what you group; HAVING = what you keep', 'SELECT aliases invisible to HAVING in most engines', 'HAVING COUNT(*) > 1 is the duplicate finder'], commonMistakes: ['Putting a plain column filter in HAVING and slowing grouping', 'Referencing SELECT aliases inside HAVING'], interviewTips: ['Answer with the evaluation-order argument first', 'Show a HAVING with two aggregate conditions'] },
  { topic: 'ORDER BY', title: 'ORDER BY and Sorting', content: 'ORDER BY sorts the final result: ORDER BY salary DESC, name ASC sorts by salary descending with name as tie-breaker - ties are resolved left to right through the key list. You can sort by column, by SELECT alias, by position (ORDER BY 2 - discouraged, fragile), or by expressions (ORDER BY salary * 12). Collation determines string comparison, and per the set-semantics rule, rows have NO guaranteed order without ORDER BY - results from quick runs are coincidental, and interviews punish relying on it.\n\nNULL ordering is engine-specific and a favourite quiz: PostgreSQL and Oracle default NULLS LAST for ASC; MySQL and SQL Server put NULLs first for ASC - and the syntax NULLS FIRST/NULLS LAST makes intent explicit where supported. The stable-sort question: if the sort key has ties, the order of tied rows is undefined - add a unique tie-breaker (ORDER BY dept, id) to make pagination deterministic, otherwise OFFSET pages can repeat or skip rows.\n\nPerformance: sorting large sets spills to disk (external merge sort), which is why LIMIT n + ORDER BY is not free - the engine still must fully sort, though top-n heap optimisations help. An index whose column order matches the ORDER BY (and direction for single-direction queries) returns rows already ordered, eliminating the sort step entirely - mixed ASC/DESC needs matching index sort orders on modern engines. Interview framing: multi-key semantics, no-order-without-ORDER-BY, NULL placement per engine, then index-matched sorting and deterministic pagination.', keyPoints: ['Left-to-right key precedence for ties', 'No guaranteed row order without ORDER BY', 'NULLS FIRST/LAST differs by engine default', 'Add a unique tie-breaker for deterministic pagination', 'Matching index order eliminates the sort step'], commonMistakes: ['Using ORDER BY 2 positional references in real code', 'Paging with OFFSET over a non-unique sort and losing/duplicating rows'], interviewTips: ['State NULL default orders for two engines from memory', 'Explain why an index can make ORDER BY free'] },
  { topic: 'LIKE', title: 'LIKE and Pattern Matching', content: 'LIKE matches string patterns with two wildcards: % (zero or more characters) and _ (exactly one). WHERE name LIKE A% finds names starting with A; %son finds ending; %a% contains; _a% finds second-letter-a. Case sensitivity depends on collation: PostgreSQL LIKE is case-sensitive with ILIKE for insensitive; MySQL default collations are case-insensitive; SQL Server depends on the column collation - stating this nuance is the difference between a junior and a solid answer. NOT LIKE negates, ESCAPE lets you match literal % or _ (LIKE 50\\%% ESCAPE \\).\n\nIndex behaviour is the interview core: LIKE term% (prefix search) can use a B-tree index just like a range scan; LIKE %term or %term% cannot - it degrades to a full scan because the index is ordered by prefix. Fixes for contains-search at scale: trigram indexes (pg_trgm in PostgreSQL), full-text search engines (tsvector, Elasticsearch), or reverse-string tricks for suffix search. Remember this pattern rule and you answer half of all LIKE questions: leading wildcard = index dead, trailing wildcard = index alive.\n\nEdge cases: NULL LIKE anything is UNKNOWN (row filtered), and regular expressions (SIMILAR TO, REGEXP_LIKE, ~ in PostgreSQL) offer more power with the same index caveat. Interview framing: the two wildcards with examples, case sensitivity per engine, then the index story with the pg_trgm/full-text escape hatch.', keyPoints: ['% = any sequence; _ = single character', 'LIKE A% is sargable; %A is not - prefix rule', 'Case sensitivity is a collation property (ILIKE, CI collations)', 'ESCAPE for literal % and _', 'NULL LIKE pattern is UNKNOWN'], commonMistakes: ['Expecting %term% to use an index', 'Assuming LIKE case behaviour is universal across engines'], interviewTips: ['Lead with the prefix-vs-leading-wildcard index rule', 'Offer trigram or full-text search as the contains-search fix'] },
  { topic: 'INNER JOIN', title: 'INNER JOIN', content: 'INNER JOIN returns only rows where the join condition matches in BOTH tables: SELECT e.name, d.name FROM emp e INNER JOIN dept d ON e.dept_id = d.id - employees without departments and departments without employees vanish. It is the default JOIN (the keyword INNER is optional). Join predicates go in ON, plain row filters in WHERE - modern optimisers treat ON and WHERE identically for INNER joins, but keeping join logic in ON documents intent and matters hugely once you switch to outer joins.\n\nMulti-predicate joins are normal: ON e.dept_id = d.id AND e.status = active combines matching and filtering in the join. Joins that produce duplicate rows: if both sides have multiple matches, the output multiplies (each emp row x each matching dept row) - row-count explosion plus needing DISTINCT is the classic smell of a missing or too-loose join condition. Self-joins (emp joined to emp ON e.manager_id = m.id) are INNER joins on the same table with aliases, the standard way to model hierarchies.\n\nExecution engines matter for the seniority signal: nested loop (probe inner per outer row - fine with small outer or indexed inner), hash join (build hash table on the smaller side, probe with larger - great for large unsorted inputs), merge join (both sides sorted on the key - efficient for pre-sorted or large data). The optimiser picks, but you should be able to name and compare all three. Interview framing: semantics with the missing-rows observation, ON vs WHERE habit, the multiplication pitfall, then the three join algorithms.', keyPoints: ['Only matching rows from both sides survive', 'ON for join predicates; WHERE for row filters', 'M x N matches multiply output rows', 'Self-join with aliases models hierarchies', 'Algorithms: nested loop, hash, merge'], commonMistakes: ['Adding DISTINCT to hide a multiplying join instead of fixing it', 'Joining without indexing the foreign key - hash joins hide it, nested loops die'], interviewTips: ['Explain when the optimiser picks hash vs nested loop', 'Show a self-join for manager hierarchy from memory'] },
  { topic: 'LEFT JOIN', title: 'LEFT (OUTER) JOIN', content: 'LEFT JOIN returns ALL rows from the left table, with matching right-table columns or NULL where no match exists: SELECT e.name, d.name FROM emp e LEFT JOIN dept d ON e.dept_id = d.id keeps department-less employees with NULL dept. It answers existence-and-missing questions, which is why it is the most interviewed join. The killer pattern - find left rows with NO match: append WHERE d.id IS NULL (the anti-join); find all with match flag: COUNT(d.id) or CASE WHEN d.id IS NULL THEN 0 ELSE 1 END.\n\nThe ON vs WHERE difference is REAL for outer joins and a favourite trick question: a predicate in ON filters which RIGHT rows match but never removes left rows (unmatched left rows survive with NULLs), while the same predicate in WHERE runs after the join and CAN remove left rows - so LEFT JOIN ... ON ... WHERE d.name = X silently converts your outer join into an inner join unless you handle NULLs. Writing WHERE (d.name = X OR d.id IS NULL) is the deliberate version.\n\nNULL multiplication follows: a left row matching N right rows appears N times - aggregating after a LEFT JOIN can double-count left-side metrics; pre-aggregate in a subquery/CTE or use EXISTS when you only need existence. RIGHT JOIN is LEFT JOIN with sides swapped (rarely written; rewrite as LEFT for readability). Interview framing: semantics, the anti-join pattern, the ON-vs-WHERE conversion trap, then the double-count warning.', keyPoints: ['All left rows kept; unmatched right columns are NULL', 'WHERE d.id IS NULL = anti-join (no match exists)', 'Predicates in ON never drop left rows; in WHERE they can', 'WHERE right.col = X turns LEFT JOIN into INNER', 'M:N multiplication double-counts in aggregates'], commonMistakes: ['Putting right-table filters in WHERE and losing outer semantics', 'SUM after LEFT JOIN counting duplicated left rows'], interviewTips: ['Demonstrate the anti-join and the conversion trap in one breath', 'Recommend pre-aggregation in a CTE for join aggregates'] },
  { topic: 'RIGHT JOIN', title: 'RIGHT (OUTER) JOIN', content: 'RIGHT JOIN keeps ALL rows from the right table with NULLs for non-matching left columns: SELECT e.name, d.name FROM emp e RIGHT JOIN dept d ON e.dept_id = d.id lists every department, including empty ones with NULL employee. It is exactly LEFT JOIN with the operands swapped - SELECT ... FROM a RIGHT JOIN b equals FROM b LEFT JOIN a - which is why most style guides ban RIGHT JOIN in favour of consistently readable LEFT JOINs; when five joins chain left to right, mixing directions makes the data flow hard to trace.\n\nWhere RIGHT JOIN does appear: when you add a join at the END of a long chain and the new table is the one that must be fully kept, some authors reach for RIGHT JOIN to avoid reordering - readability still argues for rewriting. The semantics to state precisely: unmatched right rows appear once with NULLs everywhere on the left side, matched right rows appear once per matching left row (multiplication rule identical to LEFT), and the WHERE-clause trap applies mirrored: filtering a LEFT column in WHERE can eliminate the very NULL-extended rows the outer join preserved.\n\nInterview variants worth preparing: produce departments WITH and WITHOUT employees in one query (LEFT JOIN + CASE), or count employees per department including zero-count departments (LEFT JOIN from dept side + COUNT(e.id) - COUNT(*) would return 1 for empty departments because the NULL-extended row still exists; that COUNT(*) vs COUNT(e.id) detail is the sharpest RIGHT/LEFT JOIN question asked). Interview framing: equivalence to LEFT with swap, style-guide preference, then the counting-employees-per-department trap.', keyPoints: ['Keeps all RIGHT rows; NULLs for missing left matches', 'RIGHT JOIN a,b == LEFT JOIN b,a - pure swap', 'Multiplication and WHERE traps mirror LEFT JOIN', 'Style: prefer LEFT JOIN for chained-readability', 'COUNT(e.id) not COUNT(*) for zero-count groups'], commonMistakes: ['Using COUNT(*) after an outer join and reporting 1 for empty groups', 'Mixing LEFT and RIGHT in long chains hurting readability'], interviewTips: ['State the swap equivalence, then explain the style preference', 'Solve count-employees-per-department with the COUNT(e.id) nuance'] },
  { topic: 'FULL JOIN', title: 'FULL (OUTER) JOIN', content: 'FULL OUTER JOIN returns every row from BOTH sides, matching where possible and NULL-filling the unmatched remainder: SELECT e.name, d.name FROM emp e FULL OUTER JOIN dept d ON e.dept_id = d.id shows employees without departments AND departments without employees in one result. It is the union of LEFT and RIGHT outer joins - the whole truth of both tables against the join condition.\n\nMySQL does not implement FULL OUTER JOIN (a famous gap) - the portable emulation is LEFT JOIN ... UNION ... RIGHT JOIN with an anti-join filter to avoid double-counting the matched middle: SELECT ... FROM a LEFT JOIN b ON ... UNION SELECT ... FROM a RIGHT JOIN b ON .... UNION (not UNION ALL) is required to deduplicate the rows matched from both directions; knowing why plain UNION ALL would duplicate matches is the precise understanding interviewers probe. PostgreSQL, SQL Server, and Oracle support the syntax natively.\n\nUse cases: reconciliation reports (which accounts exist in ledger A but not B and vice versa), synchronisation diffs between two systems, and audit queries where both sides must be visible. The NULL-origin problem follows: after a FULL JOIN you often cannot tell which side a row came from - add origin flags (CASE WHEN a.id IS NULL THEN b WHEN b.id IS NULL THEN a END) or use two anti-joins instead. Interview framing: semantics, the MySQL emulation with the UNION dedup rationale, then reconciliation as the flagship use case.', keyPoints: ['Keeps all rows from both sides, NULL-filling misses', 'FULL = LEFT OUTER UNION RIGHT OUTER', 'MySQL lacks it: emulate with UNION (dedups matched middle)', 'UNION ALL emulation would double-count matches', 'Flag row origin with IS NULL CASE checks'], commonMistakes: ['Emulating with UNION ALL and duplicating matched rows', 'Forgetting MySQL support is absent and shipping a syntax error'], interviewTips: ['Write the two-query emulation and explain the dedup', 'Present a reconciliation report as the motivating example'] },
  { topic: 'Self Join', title: 'Self Join', content: 'A self join joins a table to itself using different aliases - the table plays two roles simultaneously. The canonical case is hierarchies: SELECT e.name AS employee, m.name AS manager FROM emp e LEFT JOIN emp m ON e.manager_id = m.id gives every employee with their manager, and the LEFT variant ensures the CEO (manager_id NULL) still appears. Other classic uses: finding pairs (students a JOIN students b ON a.class = b.class AND a.id < b.id - the id comparison avoids duplicate mirrored pairs and self-pairing), sibling/colleague queries, and comparing rows to later versions of themselves.\n\nThe mechanics interviewers check: aliases are mandatory (the same table twice needs two names), and each alias is an independent row source to the optimiser - a self join on a big unfiltered table is a full N x N comparison, so index the join column and filter first. Pairing with a < b (or != id to merely exclude self) controls symmetry: = plus symmetric pairs double-counts.\n\nRecursive cases extend the pattern: one level of hierarchy needs one join, N levels need N joins or a recursive CTE (WITH RECURSIVE emp_tree AS (... UNION ALL ...)) - knowing when to graduate from self join to recursive CTE is the senior-level answer. Interview framing: definition with the manager query, the pairing pattern with the a < b trick, performance notes, then the recursive CTE escalation.', keyPoints: ['Same table, two aliases, two roles', 'LEFT JOIN variant keeps NULL-manager roots', 'a.id < b.id kills mirrored and self pairs', 'Independent row sources: index the join key', 'Deep hierarchies -> recursive CTE, not N joins'], commonMistakes: ['Forgetting aliases and getting ambiguous column errors', 'Pairing with != and double-counting symmetric pairs'], interviewTips: ['Write the employee-manager LEFT JOIN cold', 'Explain the pairing symmetry trick in one sentence'] },
  { topic: 'Joins', title: 'Joins Overview', content: 'Joins combine rows from two tables on a condition - the heart of relational modelling, where normalised data is split across tables and reassembled per query. The family: INNER (only matches), LEFT/RIGHT OUTER (one side fully kept), FULL OUTER (both kept), CROSS (Cartesian product - every row x every row, no condition, used deliberately for combinations or accidentally when the ON clause is forgotten), and NATURAL (implicit on same-named columns - avoided in production because column renames silently change semantics). SELF joins use aliases to join a table to itself.\n\nTwo syntax eras: the ANSI-92 explicit style (FROM a JOIN b ON a.x = b.y) versus the old implicit style (FROM a, b WHERE a.x = b.y) - the old style risks accidental cross joins when a WHERE predicate is forgotten and cannot express outer joins cleanly, which is why explicit ANSI syntax is the standard answer. Join keys should be indexed (foreign keys especially), and the optimiser chooses between nested loop, hash and merge algorithms based on sizes, indexes and sort order.\n\nSizing the answer: an INNER join of A and B on a key returns at most |A| x |B| rows, at least 0; outer joins return at least the kept-side row count - being comfortable reasoning about output cardinality is what separates practiced engineers. Interview framing: enumerate the family with one sentence each, state ANSI-92 preference, then the three algorithms and cardinality reasoning.', keyPoints: ['INNER, LEFT/RIGHT/FULL OUTER, CROSS, NATURAL, SELF', 'ANSI-92 explicit ON syntax over comma-joins', 'CROSS = all combinations; NATURAL is fragile', 'Index the join keys, especially FKs', 'Algorithms: nested loop, hash, merge - optimiser chosen'], commonMistakes: ['Forgetting the ON clause and shipping a Cartesian product', 'Using NATURAL JOIN and breaking on column renames'], interviewTips: ['Recite the family with matched-row semantics for each', 'Estimate output row counts for a given join quickly'] },
  { topic: 'Subqueries', title: 'Subqueries', content: 'A subquery is a SELECT nested inside another statement, and the taxonomy is the interview: SCALAR subqueries return one row one column (usable wherever an expression fits: SELECT name, (SELECT MAX(salary) FROM emp) AS max_sal FROM emp); ROW subqueries return one row multiple columns; TABLE subqueries return many rows - usable with IN, ANY, ALL, EXISTS, or as derived tables in FROM (SELECT dept, AVG(sal) AS a FROM emp GROUP BY dept) d WHERE d.a > 50000, where the alias is mandatory.\n\nExecution classes: uncorrelated subqueries run ONCE, independent of the outer query (the optimiser may even materialise them), while correlated subqueries reference outer columns and conceptually re-run per outer row - that cost model is the pivot to the next question, rewriting correlated forms to joins or window functions. Placement variety: in WHERE (filtering), SELECT (per-row computed columns), FROM (derived tables / inline views), and WITH (CTEs - the named, reusable form).\n\nDialect quirks worth a line: MySQL historically could not LIMIT inside IN subqueries; older MySQL could not use the same table being updated in a subquery (wrap in a derived table). Modern optimisers flatten many subqueries into joins automatically, but complex ones remain blockers. Interview framing: the return-shape taxonomy, correlated vs uncorrelated costs, placement options, then the derived-table alias rule.', keyPoints: ['Shapes: scalar (1x1), row, table (many rows)', 'FROM subqueries are derived tables and NEED an alias', 'Uncorrelated runs once; correlated runs per outer row', 'Usable in WHERE, SELECT, FROM, WITH', 'IN/ANY/ALL/EXISTS consume table subqueries'], commonMistakes: ['Missing the derived-table alias', 'Assuming every subquery re-runs per row - only correlated ones'], interviewTips: ['Classify a given subquery by shape and correlation first', 'Rewrite one correlated example into a join to show the win'] },
  { topic: 'Correlated Subqueries', title: 'Correlated Subqueries', content: 'A correlated subquery references columns of the outer query, so it logically executes once per outer row: SELECT e.name FROM emp e WHERE e.salary > (SELECT AVG(salary) FROM emp WHERE dept_id = e.dept_id) - employees earning above their OWN department average. That per-department dependency is exactly what makes it powerful and what makes it expensive: naive execution is O(outer x subquery-scan), which is why the follow-up is always rewrite-it.\n\nThe rewrites to know: JOIN against a pre-aggregated derived table - JOIN (SELECT dept_id, AVG(salary) a FROM emp GROUP BY dept_id) d ON d.dept_id = e.dept_id WHERE e.salary > d.a - or the window function form - WHERE salary > AVG(salary) OVER (PARTITION BY dept_id) - both far cheaper at scale. NOT EXISTS correlated form is the exception that stays efficient: it becomes an anti-join with early termination per row (the subquery stops at the first match), often BETTER than NOT IN (which also breaks on NULLs).\n\nWhere correlated remains the right tool: single-row lookups (latest order per customer with EXISTS or a lateral join), and LATERAL/apply joins (CROSS APPLY, LATERAL keyword) which intentionally re-evaluate per outer row to fetch top-N per group - the legitimate, index-backed correlated pattern. Interview framing: definition and the dept-average example, the cost model, the two rewrites, then the NOT EXISTS/lateral exceptions.', keyPoints: ['References outer columns - re-runs per outer row', 'Classic: salary above own department average', 'Rewrite: pre-aggregated join or AVG() OVER window', 'NOT EXISTS is efficient (anti-join, early exit) and NULL-safe', 'LATERAL/CROSS APPLY: the intentional, indexed correlated case'], commonMistakes: ['Claiming all correlated subqueries are slow - EXISTS with index is fine', 'Using NOT IN instead of NOT EXISTS and tripping on NULLs'], interviewTips: ['Write the above-dept-average query, then rewrite it twice', 'Explain NOT EXISTS early-termination vs NOT IN NULL traps'] },
  { topic: 'EXISTS', title: 'EXISTS and NOT EXISTS', content: 'EXISTS tests whether a subquery returns ANY row - it does not care what the rows contain, only that at least one exists: SELECT c.name FROM customers c WHERE EXISTS (SELECT 1 FROM orders o WHERE o.customer_id = c.id) lists customers with orders. Convention: SELECT 1 inside EXISTS signals intent (the projection is ignored). Its execution model is the whole point - semi-join semantics with EARLY TERMINATION: the engine stops scanning the subquery at the first match, per outer row, and with an index on the subquery join column that is one tiny index probe per outer row.\n\nNOT EXISTS is the anti-join: customers WITHOUT orders. It wins over NOT IN on two axes: NULL-safety - NOT IN (subquery) returns zero rows if the subquery yields a single NULL (because x NOT IN (1, NULL) can never be TRUE under three-valued logic), while NOT EXISTS is unaffected; and efficiency - NOT IN must materialise and scan the whole list, NOT EXISTS short-circuits at the first match. Given the choice, professional SQL prefers EXISTS/NOT EXISTS over IN/NOT IN for subqueries, reserving IN for short literal lists.\n\nVariants worth naming: correlated EXISTS is the general existence test; NOT EXISTS handles double-negation questions (customers with no order over 1000) which IN-formulations cannot express; and LEFT JOIN ... IS NULL is an alternative anti-join with slightly different NULL-duplication semantics. Interview framing: semantics with SELECT 1, early-termination model, NOT EXISTS vs NOT IN on NULLs and cost, then the double-negation example.', keyPoints: ['Tests existence, ignores column values - SELECT 1 convention', 'Semi-join with early exit per outer row', 'NOT EXISTS is NULL-safe; NOT IN + NULL = empty result', 'Indexed correlated EXISTS = one probe per row', 'Expresses double-negation that IN cannot'], commonMistakes: ['Writing NOT IN for anti-joins and losing rows to NULLs', 'Selecting real columns inside EXISTS expecting them to matter'], interviewTips: ['Explain early termination and one-probe-per-row execution', 'Show the no-order-over-1000 double-negation query'] },
  { topic: 'IN', title: 'IN Operator', content: 'IN tests membership: WHERE status IN (pending, shipped, delivered) or WHERE dept_id IN (SELECT id FROM dept WHERE region = west). Two forms matter: a literal list (self-documenting, fine for small sets) and a subquery (evaluated as a semi-join in modern optimisers, usually as fast as EXISTS when the subquery is uncorrelated). NOT IN negates it - and lands in the NULL trap: if the subquery returns ANY NULL, NOT IN returns NOTHING, because the comparison chain x NOT IN (1, 2, NULL) can never be proven TRUE under three-valued logic; engines return UNKNOWN, filtering every row out. The fix is NOT EXISTS, or filtering NULLs explicitly.\n\nSemantics vs OR: WHERE a IN (1, 2, 3) is exactly a = 1 OR a = 2 OR a = 3 - pure syntax sugar, same plan. Multi-column IN (WHERE (a, b) IN ((1, 2), (3, 4))) works in most engines and pairs nicely with row constructors, though optimiser support varies. Large literal lists have real costs: parse time, plan bloat, and cache pressure - thousands of ids in one query is a design smell suggesting a temp table or join instead.\n\nThe performance nuance interviewers like: for uncorrelated subqueries, IN and EXISTS usually produce identical plans (the optimiser converts IN to a semi-join); for correlated subqueries, EXISTS with early exit can beat IN; and ANY/ALL generalize IN (= ANY over a list). Interview framing: the two forms, the NOT IN NULL trap with the exact reasoning, the OR equivalence, then large-list scaling advice.', keyPoints: ['Membership test for lists or subquery results', 'IN (list) == chained OR comparisons', 'NOT IN + NULL in subquery = zero rows - use NOT EXISTS', 'Multi-column row-constructor IN exists in most engines', 'Huge id lists: use temp tables or joins instead'], commonMistakes: ['Shipping NOT IN over nullable columns', 'Passing thousands of literal ids and blaming the database'], interviewTips: ['Prove the NOT IN NULL behaviour with a two-element example', 'State when IN and EXISTS produce the same plan'] },
  { topic: 'UNION', title: 'UNION', content: 'UNION stacks result sets of two queries vertically: columns must match in COUNT and compatible TYPES (names come from the first query). UNION removes duplicate rows across the combined result - and duplicate removal means SORT or HASH of the whole set, so UNION is materially more expensive than UNION ALL, which simply concatenates and streams. The rule of thumb to state: use UNION ALL unless deduplication is genuinely required; if duplicates are impossible anyway (disjoint key sets), UNION makes you pay for nothing.\n\nSemantics detail: UNION deduplicates ENTIRE rows, not columns; NULLs compare as equal for dedup purposes; and the ORDER BY applies to the combined result only - written once, at the end, referring to the first query column names (per-dialect quirks with expressions exist, so alias columns consistently). INTERSECT (rows in both) and EXCEPT/MINUS (rows in first, not second) complete the set-operator family and also deduplicate; their ALL variants (INTERSECT ALL) exist in standard SQL.\n\nThe classic interview use: emulating FULL OUTER JOIN in MySQL (LEFT JOIN UNION RIGHT JOIN, relying on UNION dedup) - and the classic interview trap: UNION ALL when the middle rows duplicate and counting them twice, e.g. summing customers from two campaigns UNION ALLed while some belong to both. Interview framing: vertical stacking rules, UNION vs UNION ALL cost and when each, ORDER BY placement, then the set-family and the FULL JOIN emulation.', keyPoints: ['Vertical concat; column count and types must align', 'UNION dedups via sort/hash; UNION ALL streams', 'Prefer UNION ALL unless dedup is required', 'ORDER BY at the end applies to the combined set', 'INTERSECT and EXCEPT complete the set operators'], commonMistakes: ['Defaulting to UNION and paying for pointless dedup', 'Expecting per-query ORDER BY inside a UNION - invalid'], interviewTips: ['Explain the cost difference with the sort/hash reason', 'Write the FULL OUTER JOIN emulation and note why UNION is required'] },
  { topic: 'UNION ALL', title: 'UNION ALL', content: 'UNION ALL concatenates result sets with NO deduplication: every row from query A appears, then every row from query B - duplicates preserved. It is the fast set operator: no sort, no hash, output can stream as produced, and plans parallelise trivially across branches. The default advice in code review: if you cannot name a duplicate that needs removing, use UNION ALL.\n\nWhere it shines: partitioned or sharded queries (union each shard/partition result - keys are disjoint so duplicates are impossible), combining historical and recent data from differently structured tables, per-status aggregates merged into one report, and time-series stitching. Column rules are identical to UNION: matching count, compatible types, names from the first SELECT; ORDER BY and LIMIT apply once at the end to the whole combined stream.\n\nThe traps: forgetting that duplicates survive (double-counted members across overlapping segments), and type coercion surprises - unioning INT and VARCHAR columns forces casts that can silently mangle data or fail; align types with explicit CASTs in each branch. A pattern worth knowing: UNION ALL plus a source literal column (SELECT a, src FROM t1 UNION ALL SELECT a, src2 FROM t2) gives an origin tag for post-filtering - the standard data-warehouse union technique. Interview framing: no-dedup semantics and streaming cost, disjoint-set use cases, the type-casting discipline, then the source-tag pattern.', keyPoints: ['Pure concatenation - duplicates preserved', 'No sort/hash: streams, parallelises, cheapest set op', 'Use when key sets are disjoint or dupes are wanted', 'Same column count/type rules; names from first query', 'Add a source-literal column to tag origins'], commonMistakes: ['Using UNION ALL on overlapping sets and double-counting', 'Mixing incompatible column types without explicit CAST'], interviewTips: ['Lead with why streaming beats dedup cost', 'Show the source-tagging warehouse pattern'] },
  { topic: 'Transactions', title: 'Transactions and ACID', content: 'A transaction bundles multiple operations into one all-or-nothing unit: BEGIN, the work, then COMMIT (make permanent) or ROLLBACK (undo everything). The guarantees are ACID. Atomicity - all or nothing, implemented with an UNDO log that can reverse uncommitted work. Consistency - the database moves from one valid state to another: constraints, triggers and FKs enforce it. Isolation - concurrent transactions do not see each others intermediate states, implemented with locking or MVCC. Durability - once committed, power loss cannot lose it, implemented with a WRITE-AHEAD LOG flushed to disk before the commit returns. Naming the implementation mechanism behind each letter is the interview differentiator.\n\nIsolation levels trade consistency for concurrency, defined by the anomalies they prevent. READ UNCOMMITTED - dirty reads possible (seeing uncommitted data). READ COMMITTED - no dirty reads, but non-repeatable reads (same row changes between reads in one transaction). REPEATABLE READ - also no non-repeatable reads, but phantom rows (new rows appear in repeated range queries). SERIALIZABLE - everything, at the cost of throughput. MySQL InnoDB defaults to REPEATABLE READ (with next-key locking that largely stops phantoms); PostgreSQL and Oracle default READ COMMITTED.\n\nThe other classic: deadlocks between transactions updating rows in different orders - prevention is consistent lock ordering, detection is wait-for graph cycle breaking with victim rollback. Interview framing: ACID with mechanisms, the four levels with their three anomalies in order, then deadlocks and the ordering discipline.', keyPoints: ['ACID: atomic, consistent, isolated, durable', 'Undo log = atomicity; WAL = durability', 'Anomalies: dirty read, non-repeatable read, phantom', 'Levels: RU, RC, RR, Serializable - each removes one anomaly', 'Deadlock fix: lock in a consistent global order'], commonMistakes: ['Confusing consistency (ACID) with CAP consistency', 'Mixing up non-repeatable reads (same row) with phantoms (new rows in a range)'], interviewTips: ['Name the mechanism behind each ACID letter', 'Reproduce the three anomalies with mini scenarios'] },
  { topic: 'TCL', title: 'TCL - Transaction Control Language', content: 'TCL commands manage transactions: BEGIN/START TRANSACTION opens one, COMMIT makes all its changes permanent, ROLLBACK undoes them all, and SAVEPOINT carves named checkpoints inside a transaction so you can ROLLBACK TO SAVEPOINT sp1 while keeping earlier work - partial undo inside one atomic unit. RELEASE SAVEPOINT discards a savepoint. Every DML statement runs inside SOME transaction: with autocommit on, each statement commits itself; explicit transactions group several statements.\n\nThe semantics interviewers probe: commits are DURABLE (WAL flushed) and rollbacks are ATOMIC (undo log replays), but DDL often force-commits the surrounding transaction (MySQL), breaking the grouping - know your engine. SAVEPOINTs shine in bulk loaders: savepoint per batch, on batch failure roll back to that savepoint, log the bad batch, continue - a standard ETL pattern. Nested transaction syntax in most engines is really savepoints under the hood.\n\nTwo practical notes: long transactions hold locks and MVCC versions, bloating tables and blocking vacuums/cleanups - keep transactions SHORT and never wait on user input inside one; and application-level retry logic belongs around the whole transaction (on deadlock or serialization failure, retry the unit). Interview framing: the four commands, savepoint-based ETL batching, DDL auto-commit caveat, then short-transaction discipline.', keyPoints: ['BEGIN, COMMIT, ROLLBACK, SAVEPOINT, RELEASE', 'ROLLBACK TO SAVEPOINT = partial undo inside a transaction', 'DDL often force-commits (MySQL) - check your engine', 'Autocommit wraps each statement unless you begin explicitly', 'Keep transactions short; retry deadlocks as a unit'], commonMistakes: ['Expecting ROLLBACK TO SAVEPOINT to undo everything after BEGIN', 'Holding a transaction open across user interaction'], interviewTips: ['Describe the batch-load savepoint pattern concretely', 'Explain why long transactions hurt MVCC engines'] },
  { topic: 'Constraints', title: 'Constraints', content: 'Constraints are declarative rules the ENFORces on every write - the database guaranteeing data validity regardless of which application writes. The family: NOT NULL (value required), UNIQUE (no duplicate values in the column or column set), PRIMARY KEY (NOT NULL + UNIQUE - row identity), FOREIGN KEY (referential integrity - the value must exist in the referenced table), CHECK (arbitrary boolean predicate, e.g. CHECK (salary >= 0)), and DEFAULT (value when none supplied). Named constraints give clean error messages and easy ALTER ... DROP CONSTRAINT management.\n\nThe enforcement angle: constraints fail fast with specific error codes, so application code can translate them into user-friendly validation; defence in depth means validating in the app AND constraining in the DB, because scripts, migrations and other services bypass your application layer. Deferred constraints (PostgreSQL SET CONSTRAINTS DEFERRED) check at commit instead of per statement, enabling ordering tricks like swapping unique values.\n\nThe classic sequencing problem: inserting a child row referencing a parent inserted in the same transaction - fine within one transaction (the parent is visible), but bulk loads either disable checks (dangerous, must validate manually) or order inserts parents-first. Interview framing: name all six with one-line purposes, the PK = NOT NULL + UNIQUE identity point, defence-in-depth reasoning, then deferred/sequencing nuances.', keyPoints: ['NOT NULL, UNIQUE, PRIMARY KEY, FOREIGN KEY, CHECK, DEFAULT', 'PK = NOT NULL + UNIQUE, identifies the row', 'DB-side constraints protect against ALL writers', 'Deferred constraints check at COMMIT time', 'Constraint violations fail fast with specific errors'], commonMistakes: ['Saying the application alone can guarantee integrity', 'Forgetting that UNIQUE allows multiple NULLs in most engines'], interviewTips: ['List all six with purposes in under a minute', 'Explain the UNIQUE-and-NULL engine behaviour'] },
  { topic: 'Primary Key', title: 'Primary Key', content: 'A primary key identifies each row uniquely and non-nullably - the anchor of the relational model and the target of foreign keys. Technically it is NOT NULL + UNIQUE, created as PRIMARY KEY (id) inline or as a table constraint PRIMARY KEY (id), including composite keys (PRIMARY KEY (student_id, course_id) - the classic many-to-many join table key where BOTH columns together are unique). A table has exactly one PK; additional uniqueness uses UNIQUE constraints.\n\nChoice of key is a design interview: NATURAL keys (email, SSN) reflect real-world identity but change, leak PII, and format-shift - SURROGATE keys (auto-increment id, UUID) are meaningless but stable. Auto-increment ints are compact and index-friendly; UUIDs distribute generation across nodes at the cost of 16 bytes and (v4) random insertion order that fragments B-trees - UUIDv7/time-ordered variants fix the fragmentation. The practical convention: surrogate PK plus separate UNIQUE constraints on natural identifiers.\n\nEngine-specific depth worth mentioning: in InnoDB the table IS a clustered index organised by PK, so a short, monotonic PK keeps rows in insertion order and secondary indexes store the PK as row pointer - a fat UUID PK bloats EVERY secondary index. Interview framing: definition and composite case, natural vs surrogate trade, then the InnoDB clustered-index consequence.', keyPoints: ['Row identity: NOT NULL + UNIQUE, one per table', 'Composite PKs for join tables (student_id, course_id)', 'Surrogate ids beat natural keys on stability', 'Auto-increment compact; UUIDv4 random = B-tree fragmentation', 'InnoDB clusters by PK - fat PKs bloat all indexes'], commonMistakes: ['Using emails or SSNs as PKs - they change and leak', 'Believing a table can have two primary keys (one PK, many UNIQUEs)'], interviewTips: ['Argue surrogate-over-natural with the email-change scenario', 'Explain the InnoDB secondary-index bloat consequence'] },
  { topic: 'Foreign Key', title: 'Foreign Keys and Referential Integrity', content: 'A foreign key is a column (or set) referencing the primary key or a unique key of another table, and the database enforces referential integrity: you cannot insert a child row whose parent does not exist, and deleting or changing a referenced parent is controlled. The child table holds the FK; the parent is the referenced one - naming this direction precisely is the first interview checkpoint.\n\nReferential actions define parent-change behaviour: ON DELETE CASCADE (delete children automatically - right for order_items under an order), ON DELETE SET NULL (orphan children with NULL - right for optional references like employee.department_id when a department dissolves), ON DELETE RESTRICT/NO ACTION (refuse the delete - the default, NO ACTION differing from RESTRICT only in check timing in some engines), and the same four for ON UPDATE. Choosing the action per relationship is a design question interviewers love: cascade where the child has no meaning without the parent, restrict where history must be preserved.\n\nThe operational realities: FK columns MUST be indexed for join and enforcement performance (some engines auto-create the index, MySQL InnoDB does; PostgreSQL does not - create it yourself); FK checks cost writes and complicate bulk loads, so large ETL often drops and re-adds constraints (validating afterwards); and polymorphic references (a comment pointing at either a post or a photo) CANNOT be FKs - that is one of the strongest anti-NoSQL-when-relational arguments. Interview framing: definition and direction, the four referential actions with examples, then indexing and bulk-load realities.', keyPoints: ['Child column references parent PK/unique - direction matters', 'ON DELETE: CASCADE, SET NULL, RESTRICT/NO ACTION', 'Cascade for owned children; restrict to preserve history', 'Index FK columns (InnoDB auto; PostgreSQL manual)', 'Polymorphic references cannot be FK-enforced'], commonMistakes: ['Forgetting to index FK columns in PostgreSQL', 'Cascading deletes where history must be kept'], interviewTips: ['Pick referential actions for two relationships with reasons', 'Explain why bulk loaders drop constraints and re-validate'] },
  { topic: 'Indexes', title: 'Indexes and B-Trees', content: 'An index is a separate data structure - almost always a B+ tree - mapping column values to row locations, turning full table scans (O(n)) into lookups (O(log n)). The trade: every index speeds some reads and slows every write (INSERT/UPDATE/DELETE must maintain it) and consumes disk. Clustered indexes determine physical row order (one per table - InnoDB clusters by PK); secondary/non-clustered indexes point to rows (InnoDB points to the PK, MyISAM to file offsets). Composite indexes order columns by (a, b) and obey the LEFTMOST PREFIX rule: an index on (a, b, c) serves a, a+b, a+b+c filters but not bare b.\n\nCovering indexes are the pro move: if the query needs only indexed columns, the engine answers from the index alone (Using index in MySQL EXPLAIN) - INCLUDE columns in SQL Server exist exactly for this. Selectivity decides usefulness: an index on gender (low cardinality) rarely helps; the optimiser ignores it below a selectivity threshold. Index types: B-tree (default, ranges and equality), Hash (equality only, not ordered), full-text (tokenised search), spatial (R-trees), partial/filtered indexes (rows matching a predicate - tiny and potent for hot subsets), and unique indexes doubling as constraints.\n\nExplain plans are the evidence: look for type/scan vs ref/range in MySQL, Seq Scan vs Index Scan in PostgreSQL, and key_len for composite prefix usage. Interview framing: what an index is and the B+ tree shape, clustered vs secondary, leftmost prefix with an example, then covering indexes and selectivity.', keyPoints: ['B+ tree: O(log n) lookups, extra write cost', 'Clustered = row order (one per table); secondary points to PK', 'Leftmost prefix rule for composite indexes', 'Covering index answers from the index alone', 'Low-selectivity columns rarely benefit; check EXPLAIN'], commonMistakes: ['Indexing every column and tanking write throughput', 'Expecting an index on (a, b) to serve a filter on b alone'], interviewTips: ['Draw the B+ tree with leaf-level linked ranges', 'Walk one EXPLAIN and identify the index usage'] },
  { topic: 'Views', title: 'Views', content: 'A view is a stored named query that behaves like a virtual table: SELECT from it and the DBMS substitutes the underlying query at execution time - no data is stored (standard views). Uses: simplification (hide multi-join complexity behind one clean name), security (expose only salary-band or non-PII columns to analysts, instead of table grants), stability (shield applications from schema refactors by versioning the view), and consistency (one canonical active-user definition reused everywhere).\n\nUpdatability has rules: simple single-table views without aggregates, DISTINCT, GROUP BY or set operations are usually updatable - INSERT/UPDATE/DELETE pass through to the base table; anything aggregated is read-only. WITH CHECK OPTION keeps inserts/updates through the view within the view predicate (a view of active users rejects inserting an inactive one) - a favourite interview detail. Materialised views are the opposite trade: the query result is PHYSICALLY stored and refreshed (on demand, on schedule, or incrementally in some engines) - fast reads, stale-by-design data, extra storage; ideal for dashboards over expensive aggregations. PostgreSQL has REFRESH MATERIALIZED VIEW (CONCURRENTLY), Oracle has fast-refresh MVs with logs.\n\nThe operational note: views nest (view over view) and deep nesting plus optimiser limits can degrade plans - most engines inline the definitions, so a 5-deep view stack can produce a monster query. Interview framing: virtual-table definition, the four uses with the security one first, updatability rules and CHECK OPTION, then the materialised contrast.', keyPoints: ['Stored query substituted at execution - no storage', 'Security: column/row restriction without new tables', 'Simple views updatable; aggregates are read-only', 'WITH CHECK OPTION keeps writes within the predicate', 'Materialised views: stored, refreshed, fast but stale'], commonMistakes: ['Expecting an aggregate view to accept INSERTs', 'Confusing views (always current) with materialised views (stale)'], interviewTips: ['Give the analyst-security example with column restriction', 'Contrast refresh strategies for materialised views'] },
  { topic: 'Window Functions', title: 'Window Functions', content: 'Window functions compute over a SET of related rows while KEEPING every row in the output - unlike GROUP BY which collapses groups. The frame is OVER (PARTITION BY ... ORDER BY ...): PARTITION divides rows into independent groups, ORDER orders within the partition, and an optional frame clause (ROWS BETWEEN ...) bounds the calculation. SELECT name, dept, salary, RANK() OVER (PARTITION BY dept ORDER BY salary DESC) keeps every employee row AND adds their rank - the canonical example.\n\nThe three families: ranking (ROW_NUMBER - unique 1,2,3 regardless of ties; RANK - ties share a number, next skips, 1,1,3; DENSE_RANK - ties share, no skip, 1,1,2; NTILE buckets; LEAD/LAG - next/previous row values), aggregates over windows (SUM, AVG, COUNT with moving frames - SUM(salary) OVER (ORDER BY day ROWS BETWEEN 6 PRECEDING AND CURRENT ROW) is a 7-day rolling sum) and value functions (FIRST_VALUE, LAST_VALUE, NTH_VALUE). The difference between RANK and DENSE_RANK and ROW_NUMBER on ties is a guaranteed interview probe.\n\nExecution order matters: window functions run AFTER WHERE/GROUP BY/HAVING, so they cannot appear in WHERE - filter their results via a subquery/CTE (the classic top-N-per-group: qualify by ROW_NUMBER in a CTE, then filter rn <= 3 outside). Interview framing: window vs GROUP BY with the rank example, the three families with tie behaviour, then the execution-order workaround and top-N pattern.', keyPoints: ['Compute over row sets, keep all rows - vs GROUP BY collapse', 'OVER (PARTITION BY ... ORDER BY ... [frame])', 'ROW_NUMBER vs RANK vs DENSE_RANK tie behaviour', 'LEAD/LAG for next/previous; moving frames for rolling sums', 'Cannot sit in WHERE - wrap in CTE then filter'], commonMistakes: ['Using window functions directly in WHERE - invalid', 'Mixing up RANK (gaps) with DENSE_RANK (no gaps)'], interviewTips: ['Write top-3-salary-per-department with a CTE', 'State the SQL logical execution order and where windows fall'] },
  { topic: 'CTE', title: 'Common Table Expressions (CTEs)', content: 'A CTE is a named temporary result set defined with WITH, visible for the single following statement: WITH dept_avg AS (SELECT dept_id, AVG(salary) a FROM emp GROUP BY dept_id) SELECT e.name FROM emp e JOIN dept_avg d ON e.dept_id = d.dept_id WHERE e.salary > d.a. It replaces messy nested subqueries with readable, top-down query structure, and multiple CTEs chain comma-separated, each referencing earlier ones - decomposing a 300-line query into named steps. Unlike views, CTEs are statement-scoped: no catalog entry, no reuse across statements.\n\nRECURSIVE CTEs are the headline feature: WITH RECURSIVE t AS (anchor SELECT ... UNION ALL recursive SELECT referencing t) walks hierarchies and graphs - org charts (start at the CEO, join downward, UNION ALL, track a depth column), bill-of-materials explosions, transitive closures, even date-series generation. Termination is when the recursive step yields no rows; infinite loops are guarded with depth limits or cycle detection arrays - mentioning cycle detection is the senior signal.\n\nThe performance nuance interviewers now expect: MATERIALISATION differs by engine. PostgreSQL 12+ inlines non-recursive, side-effect-free CTEs into the outer query (optimisable) but keeps RECURSIVE or multi-referenced CTEs materialised (single evaluation, possibly stale statistics); SQL Server inlines aggressively; MySQL 8 materialises CTEs unless merged. So a CTE referenced TWICE may recompute or not depending on engine - for hot paths, prefer derived tables or temp tables. Interview framing: readability purpose with the dept-average example, the recursive org-chart pattern, then the materialisation differences.', keyPoints: ['WITH name AS (...) - statement-scoped named result', 'Multiple CTEs chain and reference earlier ones', 'RECURSIVE: anchor UNION ALL step for hierarchies/graphs', 'Guard recursion with depth or cycle detection', 'Materialisation varies: PG12+ inlines simple CTEs; MySQL 8 materialises'], commonMistakes: ['Forgetting the RECURSIVE keyword for self-referencing CTEs', 'Assuming a twice-referenced CTE is computed once everywhere'], interviewTips: ['Write the recursive org-chart CTE with a depth column', 'Explain PG inlining vs materialisation with the double-reference case'] },
  { topic: 'Stored Procedures', title: 'Stored Procedures', content: 'A stored procedure is a named, precompiled block of SQL plus procedural logic (variables, IF/CASE, loops, cursors, error handling) stored IN the database and executed with CALL/EXEC. Benefits: network efficiency (one call replaces N round trips), encapsulation (business rules centralised server-side, applications just call), security (grant EXECUTE without table access; SQL injection surface shrinks because parameters are typed, not string-concatenated), and plan caching (compiled once, reused). Syntax varies: MySQL DELIMITER blocks with IN/OUT/INOUT parameters, SQL Server T-SQL with OUTPUT params and return values, PostgreSQL PL/pgSQL functions returning tables or scalars.\n\nThe costs drive the modern debate: procedural code is harder to version, test and debug than application code; it ties you to the vendor dialect (PL/SQL does not port to T-SQL); scaling logic horizontally means scaling the DATABASE, which is the hardest tier to scale; and complex logic in procedures creates the "smart database" anti-pattern that complicates migrations and sharding. The pragmatic line: procedures fit set-based bulk operations, ETL steps, and hot paths where round trips dominate; business logic that changes often lives in the application.\n\nFunctions vs procedures is the guaranteed follow-up: functions MUST return a value and are callable inside SQL expressions (often required to be deterministic for indexing), procedures return nothing or via OUT params and run as standalone statements. Interview framing: definition and four benefits, the four costs with the scaling one emphasised, then the function/procedure distinction.', keyPoints: ['Named, precompiled SQL + procedural logic, executed server-side', 'Fewer round trips, centralised rules, EXECUTE grants, plan cache', 'Typed parameters reduce SQL injection risk', 'Costs: versioning, vendor lock-in, database-tier scaling', 'Functions return values usable in SQL; procedures are statements'], commonMistakes: ['Putting fast-changing business logic in procedures', 'Assuming procedures automatically run faster than parameterised app SQL'], interviewTips: ['Argue the bulk-operation sweet spot concretely', 'Draw the procedure/function line with an example of each'] },
  { topic: 'Triggers', title: 'Triggers', content: 'A trigger is procedural code attached to a table that fires AUTOMATICALLY on events - INSERT/UPDATE/DELETE, each optionally BEFORE or AFTER, and row-level (per affected row, with NEW/OLD row access) or statement-level (once per statement). Typical uses: audit logging (AFTER UPDATE copy the OLD row to an audit table), derived-column maintenance (BEFORE INSERT set updated_at or compute a slug), enforcing complex invariants spanning columns or tables that constraints cannot express, and keeping denormalised counters in sync.\n\nThe dangers are why senior engineers treat triggers with suspicion: they execute implicitly - invisible in application code, surprising debuggers; cascading triggers (a trigger firing another) create hard-to-trace chains with multiplicative latency on bulk operations; they run inside the triggering transaction, so their cost lands on every write; and migrations or schema reviews miss them. MySQL supports only FOR EACH ROW triggers and historically one per timing/event (8.0 allows multiple); PostgreSQL supports statement and row triggers with transition tables for set-based logic.\n\nBetter alternatives when the logic is really application behaviour: explicit service-layer code, or declarative features (generated columns replace compute-on-write triggers; constraints replace invariant triggers; CDC/log-based replication replaces audit triggers at scale). The interview stance: know triggers cold, use them for mechanical, local invariants (timestamps, audits), avoid them for business rules. Interview framing: timing/event/granularity taxonomy, two legitimate uses, then the four dangers and alternatives.', keyPoints: ['Fires on INSERT/UPDATE/DELETE, BEFORE or AFTER', 'Row-level (NEW/OLD rows) vs statement-level', 'Audit trails and derived-column maintenance are the good cases', 'Implicit execution and cascading chains hurt debugging and bulk loads', 'Generated columns and CDC often replace triggers'], commonMistakes: ['Putting business rules in triggers where nobody can find them', 'Forgetting triggers run inside the write transaction - latency hits every insert'], interviewTips: ['Give the audit-table AFTER UPDATE example', 'Argue triggers vs generated columns for computed fields'] },
  { topic: 'DCL', title: 'DCL - Data Control Language', content: 'DCL controls access: GRANT grants privileges and REVOKE removes them. Privileges span system level (CREATE USER, global ALL PRIVILEGES), database level (CREATE, ALTER on a schema), table level (SELECT, INSERT, UPDATE, DELETE, REFERENCES), down to column level (GRANT SELECT (name, email) ON users TO analyst) and even row level via row-security policies (PostgreSQL RLS). Users and roles: create users with authentication, group privileges into ROLES, grant roles to users - role-based administration is the maintainable pattern; the principle of least privilege is the guiding rule. WITH GRANT OPTION lets the grantee re-grant the privilege - a delegation feature and a security hazard.\n\nThe operational patterns: applications get service accounts with ONLY the needed privileges (the web app user does not need DROP), migrations run under a separate elevated account, analysts get read-only roles over curated views rather than base tables - pairing DCL with views is the standard data-exposure design. REVOKE cascades: removing a privilege someone granted onward can revoke dependents too (engine-dependent).\n\nInterview angle: DCL vs DDL vs DML classification with examples, least-privilege service-account design, the view-plus-grant pattern for restricted data exposure, and WITH GRANT OPTION implications. Mention that in MySQL privileges live in grant tables cached in memory (FLUSH PRIVILEGES after direct manipulation), while PostgreSQL roles are cluster-wide objects - a depth point if asked.', keyPoints: ['GRANT and REVOKE manage privileges', 'Levels: system, database, table, column (and row via RLS)', 'Roles group privileges; least privilege is the rule', 'WITH GRANT OPTION delegates but risks privilege spread', 'Pair views with grants for curated read-only exposure'], commonMistakes: ['Giving applications root-equivalent accounts', 'Confusing DCL (permissions) with DDL (structure)'], interviewTips: ['Classify all four SQL sublanguages with one example each', 'Design grants for a web app in three lines'] },
  { topic: 'CREATE', title: 'CREATE Statement', content: 'CREATE defines new database objects: CREATE DATABASE/TABLE/VIEW/INDEX/PROCEDURE/FUNCTION/TRIGGER/SCHEMA. The core is CREATE TABLE: columns with data types, constraints inline or as table constraints (PRIMARY KEY, FOREIGN KEY ... REFERENCES, UNIQUE, CHECK, NOT NULL, DEFAULT), and engine options. A production-grade example: CREATE TABLE orders (id BIGINT AUTO_INCREMENT PRIMARY KEY, user_id BIGINT NOT NULL, total DECIMAL(10,2) NOT NULL DEFAULT 0 CHECK (total >= 0), status ENUM(new, paid, shipped) NOT NULL DEFAULT new, created_at TIMESTAMP DEFAULT CURRENT_TIMESTAMP, FOREIGN KEY (user_id) REFERENCES users(id) ON DELETE RESTRICT) - every element earns its place.\n\nThe operational details interviewers probe: IF NOT EXISTS makes scripts idempotent; CREATE TABLE AS SELECT (CTAS) clones structure WITH data (behaviour differs per engine on indexes/constraints - usually NOT copied); temporary tables (CREATE TEMPORARY TABLE) are session-scoped scratch space; and LIKE clones structure only. Types matter more than beginners think: VARCHAR over CHAR for variable text, DECIMAL for money (never FLOAT), appropriate integer widths, and explicit character sets/collations for multi-language data.\n\nModern workflow: schema changes in production should go through MIGRATIONS - versioned, reviewed, reversible scripts rather than ad-hoc CREATEs, keeping every environment identical. Interview framing: the object list, one full CREATE TABLE annotated, idempotency and CTAS nuances, then the migration discipline.', keyPoints: ['CREATE defines tables, views, indexes, procedures, triggers', 'Inline vs table-level constraints; FKs need the parent first', 'IF NOT EXISTS for idempotent scripts', 'CTAS copies data but (usually) not indexes/constraints', 'DECIMAL for money; explicit collations for i18n'], commonMistakes: ['Using FLOAT for currency and hitting rounding drift', 'Forgetting ON DELETE behaviour when adding FKs'], interviewTips: ['Write the annotated orders table from memory', 'Explain what CTAS does and does not copy'] },
  { topic: 'ALTER', title: 'ALTER Statement', content: 'ALTER modifies existing object structure: ADD COLUMN, DROP COLUMN, MODIFY/ALTER COLUMN (type or nullability), RENAME (column or table), ADD/DROP CONSTRAINT, ADD/DROP INDEX. The catch is that on big tables ALTER is not a metadata tweak - it may rewrite the entire table. Engines differ sharply: MySQL InnoDB supports online DDL with ALGORITHM=INPLACE, LOCK=NONE for many operations (adding an index builds in background), but some (column type changes) still rebuild; PostgreSQL added columns cheaply (metadata-only with NULL/default since PG11) and has many fast paths now.\n\nThe production playbook for risky changes: perform ALTERs in maintenance windows, or use the online tools (gh-ost, pt-online-schema-change) that shadow-copy the table, sync with triggers/binlog and swap atomically; batch data backfills (UPDATE ... LIMIT loops instead of one monster transaction). Adding a NOT NULL column with a DEFAULT on a billion-row table is the classic example that used to lock everything - PG11+ makes it instant because the default is virtual.\n\nSafe sequences to know: adding an FK requires the parent to exist and validates existing rows (in PG: add NOT VALID, then VALIDATE CONSTRAINT later to avoid long locks); dropping a column should be phased (stop using it in code, then drop) so old deploys do not crash. Interview framing: the operations list, the rewrite-vs-metadata distinction with engine differences, then the online-migration toolkit and phased-drop pattern.', keyPoints: ['ALTER: add/drop/modify columns, rename, constraints, indexes', 'Big-table ALTERs may rewrite the whole table', 'MySQL INPLACE vs rebuild; PG11+ instant default columns', 'gh-ost / pt-osc for online schema changes at scale', 'Phase risky changes; NOT VALID + VALIDATE for FKs'], commonMistakes: ['Assuming ALTER TABLE ADD COLUMN is always instant', 'Running type changes on billion-row tables in one transaction'], interviewTips: ['Explain metadata-only vs table-rebuild with the PG11 case', 'Sketch how gh-ost swaps tables with minimal downtime'] },
  { topic: 'DROP', title: 'DROP Statement', content: 'DROP removes an object and its definition entirely: DROP TABLE/ DATABASE/ INDEX/ VIEW/ PROCEDURE. It is DDL, it is AUTOCOMMIT in many engines (MySQL DDL commits the surrounding transaction implicitly - not rollback-safe there; PostgreSQL wraps DDL in transactions, so a DROP inside a transaction CAN roll back - a genuinely interview-worthy difference), and it is irreversible without backups. DROP TABLE removes data AND schema; DELETE removes rows but keeps the table; TRUNCATE empties the table fast but keeps schema - knowing the triangle of the three is the classic question.\n\nSafety mechanics: IF EXISTS prevents errors on idempotent cleanup scripts; DROP DATABASE cascades to every object inside; dependent objects matter - dropping a table that other tables reference via FK fails unless you drop the constraints first; and in managed systems, dropped data may still exist in backups, snapshots and replicas until they roll past - point-in-time recovery is the real undo.\n\nOperational hygiene: prefer disabling access (REVOKE) over DROP when unsure; take a backup or a rename-shadow (RENAME TABLE x TO x_deleted) before dropping at scale - renames are instant and reversible, drops are not; and keep DROPs out of application code paths entirely - they belong in reviewed migrations only. Interview framing: DROP vs DELETE vs TRUNCATE with speed/scope/rollback axes, transactional-DDL engine difference, then the rename-before-drop pattern.', keyPoints: ['DROP removes object + data + definition; irreversible', 'DDL autocommits in MySQL; transactional DDL in PostgreSQL', 'DROP vs DELETE (rows, logged) vs TRUNCATE (all rows, fast)', 'IF EXISTS for idempotent scripts; FKs block drops', 'Rename-shadow before drop: instant and reversible'], commonMistakes: ['Believing ROLLBACK undoes DROP in MySQL', 'Dropping tables still referenced by foreign keys'], interviewTips: ['Compare DROP/DELETE/TRUNCATE on scope, speed, rollback', 'Explain the rename-then-drop deployment pattern'] },
  { topic: 'DELETE', title: 'DELETE Statement', content: 'DELETE removes rows matching a WHERE predicate, one by one, fully logged - every deleted row is written to the transaction log, so deleting millions of rows is slow, log-heavy and replication-delaying, but transactional: ROLLBACK restores. Without WHERE it empties the table row by row (use TRUNCATE for that intent). The patterns to master: DELETE with subquery (DELETE FROM orders WHERE user_id IN (SELECT id FROM users WHERE banned = 1)), self-join deletes (remove duplicates keeping the smallest id: DELETE t1 FROM t t1 JOIN t t2 ON t1.key = t2.key AND t1.id > t2.id), and EXISTS-based deletes - all standard interview exercises.\n\nThe MySQL gotcha: you cannot DELETE from a table and SELECT from the same table in a subquery directly - wrap the subquery in a derived table. Also know soft delete: instead of removing, set deleted_at = now() and filter WHERE deleted_at IS NULL everywhere (or via views/ORM scopes) - preserves audit history and FK integrity, at the cost of every query carrying the filter and indexes including deleted_at. Purges of soft-deleted rows become periodic batch DELETEs.\n\nBatching large deletes is the production skill: delete in chunks (WHERE id BETWEEN ... LIMIT 1000 in a loop), watching replication lag and lock contention - one multi-million-row DELETE in a single transaction blocks, bloats undo space and can kill replicas. Interview framing: logged-row-by-row semantics with the rollback guarantee, the self-join dedup pattern, soft-delete trade, then chunked purging.', keyPoints: ['Row-level, fully logged, rollback-safe, needs WHERE', 'Self-join delete removes duplicates keeping min id', 'MySQL: derived-table wrapper for same-table subqueries', 'Soft delete via deleted_at: history kept, filter everywhere', 'Purge in bounded chunks to protect replication and locks'], commonMistakes: ['Running a multi-million-row DELETE as one transaction', 'Forgetting the soft-delete filter in some queries'], interviewTips: ['Write the duplicate-removal self-join cold', 'Argue soft vs hard delete with an audit example'] },
  { topic: 'UPDATE', title: 'UPDATE Statement', content: 'UPDATE changes column values of rows matching WHERE: UPDATE users SET email = new, updated_at = CURRENT_TIMESTAMP WHERE id = 7. Multi-column SET in one statement; expressions may reference the row itself (SET salary = salary * 1.1); and UPDATE ... FROM / UPDATE ... JOIN (PostgreSQL FROM, MySQL UPDATE t1 JOIN t2) pulls values from other tables - the correlated update (SET total = (SELECT SUM(...) ...)) is the interview staple. ORDER BY + LIMIT on UPDATE (MySQL) enables batched updates.\n\nThe safety mechanics: run the SELECT version of the WHERE first and compare counts; wrap in a transaction with a final review before COMMIT; and beware the forgotten WHERE. Note matched vs CHANGED rows in MySQL when values are identical - affected-rows semantics is a subtle probe. Optimistic concurrency: add AND updated_at = old_value to the WHERE so two concurrent editors cannot clobber each other - the one-line answer to lost updates; zero rows changed means someone else won.\n\nBulk updates need the same chunking as deletes: one transaction per bounded batch, watching lag and lock queues; and hot-row updates (UPDATE counters SET n = n + 1 WHERE id = popular_id) serialise on one row lock - fix with sharded counters or async aggregation. Interview framing: basic + expression + join forms, the count-check/transaction discipline, optimistic-locking predicate, then hot-row contention.', keyPoints: ['SET multiple columns; expressions can reference the row', 'UPDATE ... JOIN/FROM for cross-table updates', 'Count-check + transaction before committing bulk updates', 'Optimistic lock: AND updated_at = old value in WHERE', 'Chunk massive updates; hot rows serialise on locks'], commonMistakes: ['Missing WHERE and updating the whole table', 'Assuming affected rows equals matched rows in MySQL'], interviewTips: ['Show the optimistic-lock predicate and name the lost-update problem', 'Explain why counter updates become bottlenecks'] },
  { topic: 'INSERT', title: 'INSERT Statement', content: 'INSERT adds rows: single-row (INSERT INTO users (name, email) VALUES (Ana, a@x.com) - always name the columns so the statement survives schema reordering), multi-row VALUES lists (one statement, one parse, far faster than looping singles), and INSERT ... SELECT for set-based copies (INSERT INTO archive SELECT * FROM orders WHERE created_at < cutoff). AUTO_INCREMENT/identity columns are usually omitted and generated; fetching the new id returns LAST_INSERT_ID() in MySQL, RETURNING id in PostgreSQL - the ORM identity-map behaviour underneath.\n\nConflict handling is the interview core: INSERT ... ON DUPLICATE KEY UPDATE (MySQL) and INSERT ... ON CONFLICT DO UPDATE / DO NOTHING (PostgreSQL, where the conflict target names the constraint) implement UPSERT - insert if absent, update if present - the primitive behind idempotent sync jobs and counters (SET n = n + 1 in the update branch). INSERT IGNORE (MySQL) skips conflicts and some data errors silently - convenient and dangerous; note that ON DUPLICATE KEY UPDATE consumes an AUTO_INCREMENT value even when it updates, burning ids.\n\nBulk-loading discipline: huge multi-row VALUES lists hit max_allowed_packet and statement size limits - chunk to hundreds or thousands of rows; LOAD DATA / COPY (PostgreSQL) is the real bulk path, an order of magnitude faster than INSERTs; and wrapping batches in transactions beats per-row autocommit. Unique-violation behaviour matters: a duplicate aborts the whole statement (in PG the whole transaction by default) - hence ON CONFLICT. Interview framing: the three forms with column-naming discipline, upsert syntax in two dialects, then bulk-load strategies.', keyPoints: ['Always name columns in INSERT', 'Multi-row VALUES and INSERT...SELECT for bulk', 'UPSERT: ON DUPLICATE KEY UPDATE vs ON CONFLICT DO UPDATE', 'RETURNING / LAST_INSERT_ID fetch generated keys', 'Chunk big batches; COPY/LOAD DATA for true bulk loads'], commonMistakes: ['Relying on column order without naming columns', 'Catching unique violations by INSERT IGNORE instead of ON CONFLICT'], interviewTips: ['Write an upsert counter in both MySQL and PG syntax', 'Explain why single-row autocommit inserts are slow'] },
  { topic: 'BETWEEN', title: 'BETWEEN Operator', content: 'BETWEEN is an INCLUSIVE range test: WHERE price BETWEEN 10 AND 20 matches 10 and 20 exactly - the boundary inclusion surprises people; it is shorthand for price >= 10 AND price <= 20. NOT BETWEEN negates. It works on numbers, strings (lexicographic: name BETWEEN A AND M) and dates - but with dates the inclusiveness bites: created_at BETWEEN 2024-01-01 AND 2024-01-31 with a TIMESTAMP column EXCLUDES everything after midnight on the 31st, because 2024-01-31 coerces to 2024-01-31 00:00:00. The robust patterns: BETWEEN 2024-01-01 AND 2024-01-31 23:59:59 (fragile at sub-second precision), or the better half-open form created_at >= 2024-01-01 AND created_at < 2024-02-01.\n\nIndex behaviour: a range predicate on an indexed column uses the index for the range but stops SEEKING at the first range condition - subsequent columns of a composite index cannot be seeked after a range (leftmost-prefix plus range-ends-seek rule: index on (status, created_at) with WHERE status = ? AND created_at BETWEEN ? seeks both; index on (created_at, status) with the same predicate seeks only created_at) - column ORDER in the index matters around ranges.\n\nThe swap trick to remember: reversing bounds (BETWEEN 20 AND 10) returns nothing silently. Interview framing: inclusive semantics with the number example, the timestamp-midnight trap and the half-open fix, then the composite-index interaction with ranges.', keyPoints: ['Inclusive on both ends: >= low AND <= high', 'Works on numbers, strings, dates', 'Timestamp BETWEEN date AND date drops the last day - use >= and <', 'Range predicates end index seeking on composite indexes', 'Reversed bounds silently return empty'], commonMistakes: ['Forgetting both endpoints are included', 'Using BETWEEN on datetimes without the time component check'], interviewTips: ['Show the January-end timestamp trap and fix', 'Explain why the range column should come after equality columns in an index'] },
  { topic: 'CASE', title: 'CASE Expressions', content: 'CASE is SQLs if/else EXPRESSION - it produces a VALUE, usable anywhere expressions fit: SELECT lists, WHERE, ORDER BY, HAVING, even inside aggregates. Two forms: searched (CASE WHEN cond1 THEN r1 WHEN cond2 THEN r2 ELSE r3 END - the general form) and simple (CASE status WHEN new THEN 1 WHEN paid THEN 2 END - equality dispatch). Rules: exactly one branch evaluates; without ELSE, non-matching rows get NULL; all THEN results must be type-compatible.\n\nThe flagship uses: conditional aggregation (SUM(CASE WHEN status = paid THEN amount ELSE 0 END) AS paid_total - or COUNT(CASE WHEN ... THEN 1 END) counting only non-NULLs; FILTER (WHERE ...) in PostgreSQL is the modern equivalent), PIVOT emulation (rows to columns with one SUM(CASE...) per output column), sorting by custom logic (ORDER BY CASE priority WHEN high THEN 1 ... END), and in WHERE, guarding logic like CASE WHEN @all THEN 1 = 1 ELSE id = @id END - though that pattern often defeats index usage and parameter sniffing; OR handling in the application is usually better.\n\nOrder matters: WHEN branches evaluate top-down, so the FIRST match wins - overlapping conditions silently take the earlier branch, a classic bug. Nested CASE is legal but unreadable; prefer Boolean composition. Interview framing: expression-not-statement framing with both syntaxes, conditional aggregation and pivot examples, top-down evaluation semantics, then the WHERE-CASE index caveat.', keyPoints: ['CASE returns a value - usable in any expression slot', 'Searched (WHEN cond) vs simple (CASE col WHEN val) forms', 'Conditional aggregation and PIVOT emulation', 'First matching WHEN wins - order the branches deliberately', 'No ELSE means NULL, not zero'], commonMistakes: ['Forgetting ELSE and treating NULL results as 0', 'Overlapping WHEN conditions evaluated in the wrong order'], interviewTips: ['Write the paid/unpaid totals conditional aggregation cold', 'Build a 3-column pivot from a long-format table'] },
  { topic: 'UNIQUE', title: 'UNIQUE Constraint', content: 'UNIQUE guarantees no duplicate values in a column or column COMBINATION - the database rejects any INSERT or UPDATE that would create a duplicate. It is implemented as a unique index (the constraint and the index are the same structure), which means it also SPEEDS UP lookups on those columns - every unique constraint is a free index. Unlike PRIMARY KEY, UNIQUE columns generally allow NULLs, and the NULL rule matters: standard SQL and PostgreSQL allow MULTIPLE NULLs (NULL != NULL), MySQL likewise, while SQL Server treats NULLs as equal (one NULL max) - and PostgreSQL 15+ adds NULLS NOT DISTINCT to change that. Composite UNIQUE (UNIQUE (user_id, course_id)) constrains the PAIR: the same user cannot enrol twice, but two different users can share a course_id value.\n\nDesign uses: natural-key enforcement alongside a surrogate PK (UNIQUE (email) with PK id - the standard combo), join-table duplicate prevention (UNIQUE (follower_id, followee_id) makes a follow idempotent), and partial unique indexes for state rules (CREATE UNIQUE INDEX ... ON orders (user_id) WHERE status = active - one active cart per user, expressible ONLY with a partial index, a favourite PostgreSQL depth point).\n\nError handling: violations raise a specific error (1062 MySQL, 23505 PostgreSQL) which application code should catch and translate - or pre-empt with ON CONFLICT upserts. Interview framing: constraint-vs-PK contrast with NULL semantics, composite form for join tables, then the partial-index trick and the free-index benefit.', keyPoints: ['No duplicates in a column or column set; backed by a unique index', 'Standard engines allow multiple NULLs (NULLS NOT DISTINCT changes it)', 'Composite UNIQUE constrains the pair, not each column', 'Partial unique index: one active row per user', 'Doubles as a lookup index - constraint plus performance'], commonMistakes: ['Assuming UNIQUE rejects NULLs like PK does', 'Putting UNIQUE on each column when the PAIR must be unique'], interviewTips: ['Give the enrolment join-table composite example', 'Explain the one-active-cart partial unique index'] },
  { topic: 'NOT NULL', title: 'NOT NULL Constraint', content: 'NOT NULL forbids NULL in a column, forcing every row to carry a real value - the simplest and most underrated data-quality constraint. Its biggest practical win is index and optimiser friendliness: NOT NULL columns eliminate the three-valued-logic minefield (NULL comparisons yield UNKNOWN, so x != 5 silently excludes NULL rows too), make COUNT(col) meaningful, and let engines use simpler plans (NOT IN with NULLs becomes safe, UNIQUE semantics simplify). Defaults pair with it: NOT NULL DEFAULT 0 for counters, NOT NULL DEFAULT CURRENT_TIMESTAMP for created_at.\n\nThe NULL semantics interview set: NULL = NULL is UNKNOWN, not TRUE - so WHERE x = NULL never matches (use IS NULL); NULL propagates through arithmetic (NULL + 1 = NULL); aggregates ignore NULLs except COUNT(*); and NOT NULL plus DEFAULT removes the ambiguity at the source. Adding NOT NULL to an existing column with NULLs fails - the migration sequence is: add default, backfill NULLs in batches, then SET NOT NULL (in PG: CHECK (col IS NOT NULL) NOT VALID then VALIDATE, then SET NOT NULL, then drop the CHECK - the lock-minimising dance).\n\nDesign stance: columns should be NOT NULL by default; make a column nullable only when absence is genuinely a STATE (middle name, optional manager) - and prefer DEFAULTs over NULL sentinels (empty string vs NULL is a classic mess; pick one convention). Interview framing: the constraint plus its optimiser benefits, the NULL-comparison semantics set, then the migration sequence.', keyPoints: ['Forbids NULL; pair with DEFAULT for new rows', 'Removes three-valued-logic edge cases (x != 5 skips NULLs)', 'NULL = NULL is UNKNOWN; use IS NULL', 'Backfill before SET NOT NULL; PG trick with NOT VALID CHECK', 'Default to NOT NULL; nullable only for real absence-of-state'], commonMistakes: ['Writing WHERE col = NULL instead of IS NULL', 'Adding NOT NULL to a column that still has NULLs'], interviewTips: ['Demonstrate the x != 5 NULL exclusion with rows', 'Recite the safe ALTER sequence for backfill-and-constrain'] },
  { topic: 'CHECK', title: 'CHECK Constraint', content: 'CHECK enforces a Boolean PREDICATE on column or row values: CHECK (salary >= 0), CHECK (end_date > start_date), CHECK (status IN (new, paid, shipped)) - arbitrary row-level rules that NOT NULL and types cannot express. It runs on every INSERT and UPDATE affecting the row; the write fails with a specific error if the predicate is false or UNKNOWN (NULL results PASS a CHECK - a NULL salary does not violate CHECK (salary >= 0), another reason NOT NULL and CHECK pair up). Inline (column-level, referencing just that column) or table-level (referencing multiple columns, like the date comparison).\n\nEngine history is a favourite probe: MySQL PARSED but IGNORED CHECK constraints before 8.0.16 (validation had to live in triggers or app code); PostgreSQL enforced them all along (with the NOT VALID/VALIDATE two-step for avoiding long locks on big tables - add the constraint NOT VALID, backfill bad rows, VALIDATE with only a brief lock). SQL Server enforces with WITH NOCHECK option for legacy data.\n\nThe design division of labour: CHECK handles local invariants (ranges, cross-column comparisons); FK handles relationships; UNIQUE handles identity; application code handles cross-table business rules the schema cannot see. The enum-as-CHECK vs lookup-table debate: CHECK (status IN (...)) is simple but requires an ALTER to extend; a status lookup table extends with data and gives FK enforcement - the scalable choice for evolving domains. Interview framing: definition with three examples, NULL-passes semantics, the MySQL history, then the CHECK-vs-lookup-table trade.', keyPoints: ['Row-level Boolean predicates enforced on writes', 'NULL results pass CHECK - pair with NOT NULL', 'Column-level vs table-level (multi-column) form', 'MySQL ignored CHECK before 8.0.16; PG has NOT VALID/VALIDATE', 'Extendable domains: lookup table + FK beats CHECK IN-list'], commonMistakes: ['Assuming CHECK rejects NULL values', 'Using CHECK for cross-table rules it cannot enforce'], interviewTips: ['Give the end_date > start_date table-level example', 'Explain the enum-vs-lookup-table evolution argument'] },
];
